Title: Sis' Becky's Pickaninny
Credit: Adapted from the tale by Charles W. Chesnutt (1899)
Order: 5
Production Code: 1x05

FADE IN:

EXT. A COUNTRY GRAVEYARD - NIGHT

AMB: crickets, a distant owl, the slow tick of a cooling carriage-wheel

A full moon over a little burying-ground; wooden markers, a few stones, long grass. A lantern hangs on a post.

A rabbit sits upright among the graves, ears turned toward us.

Uncle Julius McAdoo steps out from behind a cedar with a lantern in one hand. He is crossing the graveyard as another man might cross a parlor. He stops, sets the lantern down, and speaks to the lens, softly, so as not to disturb the rabbit.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Dey's folks'll sell you a rabbit foot fer a nickel. Dey ain' wuth it. De fo'-foot ain' got no power. It has ter be de hin'-foot. De lef' hin'-foot. Er a grabe-ya'd rabbit. Killt by a cross-eyed nigger on a da'k night in de full er de moon.

The rabbit turns its head, as if listening.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Now you knows why dey ain' many.

SFX: a short sharp crack, distant, and the rabbit gone

MUSIC: STING - a single high string, held

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I doan ax you ter b'lieve it. I des ax you ter set a minute.

SUPER: "THE CONJURE WOMAN"

INT. THE VINEYARD - BEDROOM - DAY

A shaded room. Annie, in a dressing-gown, sits by the window with a book open in her lap. She has not turned a page in a long time. Her face is thin. There is a cup of tea on the table beside her and it is cold.

John, in the doorway, watches her.

SUPER: "THE END OF THEIR SECOND YEAR."

DOCTOR (O.S.)
(low, in the hall)
You must keep up her spirits. This melancholy lowers her tone too much. It tends to lessen her strength, and, if it continue too long, may be fraught with grave consequences.

JOHN
(low)
I've read to her. I've had the hands up to sing to her in the evenings. Her friends come. Letters come from home.

DOCTOR (O.S.)
Nothing rouses her?

JOHN
Nothing.

Annie's eyes go to the window. She says something, to no one.

ANNIE
I keep thinking something is going to happen.

JOHN
Nothing is going to happen.

She nods and does not believe him.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - AFTERNOON

A shaded corner. John leads Annie out, settles her in an armchair filled with pillows, tucks a shawl round her knees. A bright spring afternoon. She looks at the scenery as though she had lost interest in it.

Julius comes through the yard, taking off his tattered straw hat. He has something small in his hand.

JULIUS
How is you feelin' dis atternoon, ma'm?

JOHN
She is not very cheerful, Julius.

Annie, without energy to speak, lets her eyes rest on the old man.

JOHN (CONT'D)
Sit down, if you like.

Julius takes the top step and sits. He fingers the object in his hand.

JOHN (CONT'D)
What is that you have there, Julius?

JULIUS
Dis is my rabbit foot, suh.

JOHN
What do you do with it?

JULIUS
I kyars it wid me fer luck, suh.

JOHN
(half to him and half to his wife)
Julius, your people will never rise in the world until they throw off these childish superstitions and learn to live by the light of reason and common sense. How absurd to imagine that the fore-foot of a poor dead rabbit, with which he timorously felt his way along through a life surrounded by snares and pitfalls, beset by enemies on every hand, can promote happiness or success, or ward off failure or misfortune!

ANNIE
(with faint interest)
It is ridiculous.

JULIUS
Dat 's w'at I tells dese niggers roun' heah. De fo'-foot ain' got no power. It has ter be de hin'-foot, suh,--de lef hin'-foot er a grabe-ya'd rabbit, killt by a cross-eyed nigger on a da'k night in de full er de moon.

JOHN
They must be very rare and valuable.

JULIUS
Dey is kinder ska'ce, suh, en dey ain' no 'mount er money could buy mine. I mought len' it ter anybody I sot sto' by, but I would n' sell it, no indeed, suh.

JOHN
How do you know it brings good luck?

JULIUS
'Ca'se I ain' had no bad luck sence I had it, suh, en I's had dis rabbit foot fer fo'ty yeahs. I had a good marster befo' de wah, en I wa'n't sol' erway, en I wuz sot free; en dat 'uz all good luck.

JOHN
But that doesn't prove anything. Many other people have gone through a similar experience, and probably more than one of them had no rabbit's foot.

JULIUS
Law, suh! You doan hafter prove 'bout de rabbit foot! Eve'ybody knows dat; leas'ways eve'ybody roun' heah knows it. But ef it has ter be prove' ter folks w'at wa'n't bawn en raise' in dis naberhood, dey is a' easy way ter prove it.

He turns on the step, and looks at Annie.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Is I eber tol' you de tale er Sis' Becky en her pickaninny?

JOHN
(a glance at Annie)
No. Let us hear it.

Julius settles his hat on his knee.

JULIUS
Dis yer Becky useter b'long ter ole Kunnel Pen'leton, who owned a plantation down on de Wim'l'ton Road, 'bout ten miles fum heah...

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, a hand drum

SUPER: "BEFORE THE WAR."

EXT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - COTTON FIELD - DAY (BEFORE THE WAR)

A line of women hoeing. At the head of the line, a field-hand setting the pace: Becky, strong, composed, a head-handkerchief over her hair.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dis yer Becky wuz a fiel'-han', en a monst'us good 'un. She had a husban' oncet, a nigger w'at b'longed on de nex' plantation, but de man w'at owned her husban' died, en his lan' en his niggers had ter be sol' fer ter pay his debts.

EXT. A NEIGHBORING PLANTATION - DAY

An auction in a yard: a table, a clerk, a crowd of white men, a coffle in the dust. A man in the line, her husband, looks across the road toward the Pendleton fence. He is led away.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Kunnel Pen'leton 'lowed he'd 'a' bought dis nigger, but he had be'n bettin' on hoss races, en did n' hab no money, en so Becky's husban' wuz sol' erway ter Fuhginny.

INT. BECKY'S CABIN - NIGHT

A lamp. Mose, a baby, black as a plum and bright-eyed, laughing, crawling across the floor to his mother. Becky, home from the field, scoops him up and holds him against her neck. The child crows.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Co'se Becky went on some 'bout losin' her man, but she could n' he'p herse'f; en 'sides dat, she had her pickaninny fer ter comfo't her. Dis yer little Mose wuz de cutes', blackes', shiny-eyedes' little nigger you eber laid eyes on, en he wuz ez fon' er his mammy ez his mammy wuz er him.

In the daytime, the nursery cabin: Aun' Nancy, rocking, with the plantation's babies at her feet. She takes Mose onto her lap.

EXT. RACE-TRACK - DAY

A county race; bunting; a crowd; a row of fine horses led out by their grooms. Colonel Pendleton, in a planter's coat, hat tilted back, bets everything he has on his own horse.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Kunnel Pen'leton did n' nebber hab no luck wid his hosses, ef he did keep hisse'f po' projeckin' wid 'em. But dis time dey wuz a hoss name' Lightnin' Bug, w'at b'longed ter ernudder man, en dis hoss won de sweep-stakes.

SFX: a bell, hooves, a roar

The colonel's horse is last. Lightnin' Bug wins by two lengths. The colonel stares at the winner with a look of pure longing.

He walks across the infield to a man in a black coat: The Horse Trader, calm, polite, cold-eyed. They talk across the horse's neck.

COLONEL PENDLETON
What is you willin' ter take fer 'im?

HORSE TRADER
I'll take a thousan' dollahs fer dat hoss.

COLONEL PENDLETON
I'll gib you my note fer 'leven hund'ed dollahs.

HORSE TRADER
(shaking his head)
Yo' note, suh, is better 'n gol', I doan doubt. But I is made it a rule in my bizness not ter take no notes fum nobody. Howsomeber, suh, ef you is kinder sho't er fun's, mos' lackly we kin make some kin' er bahg'in.

He lets that sit. A groom wipes the horse's flank.

HORSE TRADER (CONT'D)
En w'iles we is talkin', I mought 's well say dat I needs ernudder good nigger down on my place. Ef you is got a good one ter spar', I mought trade wid you.

The colonel, who has no negroes to spare, thinks of Lightnin' Bug.

COLONEL PENDLETON
Well, I doan lack ter, but I reckon I'll haf ter. You come out ter my plantation ter-morrer en look ober my niggers, en pick out de one you wants.

EXT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - DAY

The trader on a good horse, riding slowly along the cotton rows, glancing at the faces. At the head of a line, a strong woman with a baby tied on her back at the turn: Becky. He stops.

HORSE TRADER
(to the colonel)
I needs a noo nigger 'oman down ter my place, fer ter cook en wash. Dat young 'oman'll des fill de bill. You gimme her, en you kin hab Lightnin' Bug.

COLONEL PENDLETON
(a pained pause)
I doan lack ter trade Sis' Becky. She's nigh 'bout de bes' fiel'-han' I got. En I doan keer ter take de mammies 'way fum dey chillun w'iles de chillun is little.

HORSE TRADER
Den you doan hab de hoss.

COLONEL PENDLETON
Well. You kin hab de 'oman. But I doan lack ter sen' her 'way fum her baby. W'at'll you gimme fer dat nigger baby?

HORSE TRADER
I doan want de baby. I ain' got no use fer de baby.

COLONEL PENDLETON
I'll th'ow dat pickaninny in fer good measure.

HORSE TRADER
(shaking his head)
I's much erbleedzd, but I doan raise niggers; I raises hosses, en I doan wanter be both'rin' wid no nigger babies. Nemmine de baby. I'll keep dat 'oman so busy she'll fergit de baby; fer niggers is made ter wuk, en dey ain' got no time fer no sich foolis'ness ez babies.

The colonel is a kind man and does not like to make trouble for anybody. He nods.

EXT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - YARD - MORNING

The colonel, hat in his hand, speaks gently to Becky at the door of the cabin.

COLONEL PENDLETON
Becky, I's gwine sen' you down ter Robeson County fer a day er so, ter he'p out my son-in-law in his wuk. Dis gen'leman is gwine dat way, en he has ax' me ter let you ride 'long in his buggy.

BECKY
Kin I kyar little Mose wid me, marster?

COLONEL PENDLETON
(looking away)
N-o. I reckon you better let Aun' Nancy look atter yo' baby fer de day er two you'll be gone. She'll see dat he gits ernuff ter eat 'tel you gits back.

She kisses the baby. He puts his arms round her neck, and laughs and crows, as if it were wonderful fun for his mother to go away and leave him.

EXT. THE LUMBERTON ROAD - DAY

A buggy, two people. Becky on the seat, the trader beside her. After a few miles he turns the horse in a different direction.

SFX: wheels on sand; a sparrow's chirp in a bush

BECKY
Is we gwine ter Robeson County by a noo road?

HORSE TRADER
No, nigger. I ain' gwine ter Robeson County at all. I's gwine ter Bladen County, whar my plantation is, en whar I raises all my hosses.

BECKY
(her heart in her mouth)
But how is I gwine ter git ter Mis' Laura's plantation down in Robeson County?

HORSE TRADER
You ain' gwine ter git dere at all. You b'longs ter me now, fer I done traded my bes' race hoss fer you wid yo' ole marster. Ef you is a good gal, I'll treat you right.

She grips the seat. She does not scream. She looks back, down the road, at the dust they have made.

INT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - NURSERY - NIGHT

Little Mose in Aun' Nancy's lap, crying. She rocks him and rocks him. He cries himself to sleep. The next evening he cries again. By the third day his eyes have lost their shine and he will not eat.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De nex' day his little eyes 'mence' ter lose dey shine, en he would n' eat nuffin, en he 'mence' ter look so peaked dat Aun' Nancy tuk 'n kyared 'im up ter de big house.

Aun' Nancy, in the doorway of the big house, holds out the child to her old mistress. The mistress gives her a bottle of medicine and a doubtful look.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - DAY

Aun' Nancy, with the child in one arm and a handkerchief of green peas in the other, at a cabin door. Behind it, a tall woman, motionless. Aun' Peggy.

AUN' NANCY
I's fotch you a mess er green peas. I wants you ter look at dis chile.

Aun' Peggy takes the handkerchief and looks into it.

AUN' PEGGY
Dat is a monst'us small mess er peas you is fotch' me.

AUN' NANCY
Yas, I knows. But dis yere is a monst'us small pickaninny.

AUN' PEGGY
(a long beat)
You'll hafter fetch me sump'n mo', fer you can't 'spec' me ter was'e my time diggin' roots en wukkin' cunj'ation fer nuffin.

AUN' NANCY
All right. I'll fetch you sump'n mo' nex' time.

AUN' PEGGY
(looking at the child)
Wat dis yer little pickaninny needs is ter see his mammy. You leabe 'im heah 'tel ebenin', en I'll show 'im his mammy.

INT. AUN' PEGGY'S CABIN - EVENING

Aun' Peggy alone with the child in her lap. She works her roots over him, murmuring. The room fills with the hum of a hundred wings.

SFX: a hummingbird's drone, rising

She lifts her hands. On her palm, a tiny iridescent bird flicks into the air and darts out of the open window.

EXT. THE SKY OVER THE BLADEN ROAD - DAY

A hummingbird over pine forests, over river, over a long yellow road. It flies for a very long time.

EXT. THE HORSE TRADER'S PLANTATION - DAY

A big house, stables, paddocks full of race-horses. In the yard, bent over a wash-tub, Becky, pale, stiff, wiping her brow with the back of her arm.

SFX: a low sweet hum near her ear

She stops. She turns her head. A hummingbird hangs a foot from her face, hovering. Something in her chest loosens a little.

BECKY
(whispering)
Mose?

The bird circles her once, twice, and goes. She stands very still. She goes on with the washing, a little faster than before.

INT. BECKY'S CABIN (THE TRADER'S PLACE) - NIGHT

Becky asleep, one hand clenched in the corner of a quilt. Her lips move. In the dream, she is holding Mose, kissing his forehead.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Fer th'ee er fo' days Sis' Becky went 'bout her wuk wid mo' sperrit dan she 'd showed sence she 'd be'n down dere.

INT. AUN' PEGGY'S CABIN - DAY

Aun' Nancy, with the baby again, sagging, pale, in the doorway.

JULIUS (V.O.)
To'ds de een' er de week he 'mence' ter git res'less ag'in, en stop' eatin', en Aun' Nancy kyared 'im down ter Aun' Peggy once mo'.

Aun' Peggy lifts her hands again; this time the bird that flies out the window is a mockingbird.

EXT. THE HORSE TRADER'S PLANTATION - KITCHEN YARD - DAY

Becky, in the kitchen door, her eyes full of tears. A mockingbird lands in the tree and sings. It sings and sings, as if its throat would split. It hops down to the step. It flies to Becky's shoulder. It pecks at the piece of bread in her hand and flutters its wings against the side of her face.

BECKY
(laughing, crying)
Little Mose.

She holds out the bread. The bird eats from her hand.

SFX: a mockingbird, a full song, rising and rising

MUSIC: STING - a rising run of strings

INT. AUN' PEGGY'S CABIN - DAY

Aun' Nancy, thin and worn, at Aun' Peggy's door, with a Sunday head-handkerchief in her hand. Folded.

AUN' NANCY
Aun' Peggy, ain' dey no way you kin fetch Sis' Becky back home?

AUN' PEGGY
I dunno 'bout dat. I'll hafter wuk my roots en fin' out. It'll take a monst'us heap er wuk.

AUN' NANCY
I is fotch' you my bes' Sunday head-hankercher. Will dat do?

Aun' Peggy runs a hand over the cloth.

AUN' PEGGY
Yas, dat'll do fus'-rate. I 'lows mos' lackly I kin git Sis' Becky back, but it 's gwine take fig'rin' en studyin' ez well ez cunj'in'. De fus' thing ter do 'll be ter stop fetchin' dat pickaninny down heah, en not sen' 'im ter see his mammy no mo'.

AUN' NANCY
Yes'm.

AUN' PEGGY
'Less'n you comes fer dat, you neenter come back ter see me no mo' 'tel I sen's fer you.

She goes inside. A hornet comes out of the nest under the eaves.

EXT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - STABLE - NIGHT

A hornet drones across a moonlit yard, and settles on a long dark leg, in a stall: Lightnin' Bug's foreleg. A small sting.

MUSIC: BED IN - hornet drone, a thin, sly viola

INT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - STABLE - MORNING

The colonel at the stall door. Lightnin' Bug's knees are the size of a man's head. The colonel opens his mouth and a stream of the most inventive abuse a man ever poured on a horse trader comes out. A stable-boy goes to hide in the hay.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Hit would 'a' des made you trimble lack a leaf fer ter heah him cuss dat hoss trader.

EXT. A TREE NEAR AUN' PEGGY'S CABIN - DAY

A sparrow on a branch, watching the road toward the big house.

INT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - STUDY - DAY

The colonel, coat off, sleeves rolled, writes a letter.

COLONEL PENDLETON
(writing, aloud)
You is sol' me a hoss w'at is got a ringbone er a spavin er sump'n, en w'at I paid you fer wuz a soun' hoss. I wants you ter sen' my nigger 'oman back en take yo' ole hoss, er e'se I'll sue you, sho 's you bawn.

INT. THE HORSE TRADER'S PLANTATION - STUDY - DAY

The horse trader, in an armchair, reading the colonel's letter without any expression. He answers, in a neat copperplate hand.

HORSE TRADER
(writing, aloud)
A bahg'in wuz a bahg'in. Lightnin' Bug wuz soun' w'en I sol' 'im. Ef Kunnel Pen'leton did n' knowed ernuff 'bout hosses ter take keer er a fine racer, dat wuz his own fune'al.

INT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - STUDY - DAY

The colonel crumples the letter, and reflects on what lawsuits cost and how slow they are. He smooths the crumpled letter out, folds it, and puts it in a drawer.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Aun' Peggy knowed w'at wuz gwine on all dis time. She fix' up a little bag wid some roots en one thing en ernudder in it, en gun it ter dis sparrer er her'n, en tol' 'im ter take it 'way down yander whar Sis' Becky wuz, en drap it right befo' de do' er her cabin.

EXT. THE HORSE TRADER'S PLANTATION - CABIN - DAWN

A small bag, on the step, in the dew. Becky comes out of the door, and sees it, and stops.

JULIUS (V.O.)
One night Sis' Becky dremp' her pickaninny wuz dead. She dremp' de same dream th'ee nights runnin', en den de nex' mawnin', she foun' dis yer little bag.

She picks it up, and holds it in both hands, like a dead thing. She looks toward the big house. She doesn't throw it away.

INT. THE HORSE TRADER'S PLANTATION - CABIN - DAY

Becky in bed, small, turned to the wall.

BECKY
(low)
I's be'n cunju'd. I's gwine ter die. En ez long ez my pickaninny is dead, dey ain' no use tryin' ter do nuffin nohow.

The horse trader, at the foot of the bed, laughing, then arguing, then, as she grows worse, no longer laughing.

HORSE TRADER
Dey ain' no sech thing ez cunj'in'.

BECKY
(not turning)
I's be'n cunju'd.

He stands there, in the doorway. Then he does arithmetic. A lame horse can be doctored and sold again. A dead hand cannot.

JULIUS (V.O.)
A lame hoss wuz better 'n a dead nigger.

INT. THE HORSE TRADER'S PLANTATION - STUDY - DAY

He writes. We see the words over his shoulder.

HORSE TRADER
(writing, aloud)
My conscience has be'n troublin' me 'bout dat ringbone' hoss I sol' you. Some folks 'lows a hoss trader ain' got no conscience, but dey doan know me, fer dat is my weak spot, en de reason I ain' made no mo' money hoss tradin'. I is got so I can't sleep nights fum studyin' 'bout dat spavin' hoss. Ef you 'll sen' Lightnin' Bug down heah, I'll sen' yo' nigger 'oman back, en we'll call de trade off, en be ez good frien's ez we eber wuz, en no ha'd feelin's.

EXT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - LANE - DAY

A buggy comes up the lane: Becky, thin, looking at the house as if she were afraid to believe it. Ahead of her, in the nursery door, Aun' Nancy, with a toddler on her hip who holds out his arms and kicks.

SFX: a baby's crow, a mother's cry

Becky runs. Aun' Nancy hands the baby down. Becky holds him, shaking.

MUSIC: BED IN - strings, major, full

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - DAY

Becky, at Aun' Peggy's door, hands folded.

AUN' PEGGY
(looking her over)
I cunju'd you.

BECKY
Aun' Nancy tol' me.

AUN' PEGGY
Put yo' han' out.

She takes the bag, undoes it, and shakes out the roots. She puts her palm on Becky's forehead. The bag is gone. Becky breathes.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Aun' Peggy tuk de goopher off'n her, en she got well, en stayed on de plantation, en raise' her pickaninny.

EXT. PENDLETON PLANTATION - BIG HOUSE - NIGHT

A porch lit with lamps, the colonel's family in rocking-chairs. In the yard, a young man, lean, whistling and singing like a mockingbird. Mose, grown, shining-eyed. The family claps. They toss him coins and a piece of cake.

JULIUS (V.O.)
W'en little Mose growed up, he could sing en whistle des lack a mawkin'-bird, so dat de w'ite folks useter hab 'im come up ter de big house at night, en whistle en sing fer 'em. En dey useter gib 'im money en vittles, w'ich he alluz tuk home ter his mammy; fer he knowed all 'bout w'at she had gone th'oo.

EXT. A BLACKSMITH SHOP - DAY

A forge. Mose, in a leather apron, hammering, bright with sweat. The colonel pays him, and stands back. He hires his own time.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He tu'nt out ter be a sma't man, en l'arnt de blacksmif trade; en Kunnel Pen'leton let 'im hire his time. En bimeby he bought his mammy en sot her free, en den he bought hisse'f.

Mose, with papers in his hand, in a clerk's office. He lays a stack of money on the desk. He signs. He goes out and walks down the road to a cabin, where an older woman sits in a rocking-chair on the porch. Becky. He gives her the paper and kneels.

SFX: a mockingbird, a full song, rising

EXT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - DUSK (PRESENT)

Julius finishing; the afternoon light is going gold. Annie, sitting up. She is looking at Julius. There is color in her face.

JOHN
(clearing his throat)
That is a very ingenious fairy tale, Julius, and we are much obliged to you.

ANNIE
(sharply)
Why, John! The story bears the stamp of truth, if ever a story did.

JOHN
Yes, especially the humming-bird episode, and the mocking-bird digression, to say nothing of the doings of the hornet and the sparrow.

ANNIE
Oh, well, I don't care. Those are mere ornamental details and not at all essential. The story is true to nature, and might have happened half a hundred times, and no doubt did happen, in those horrid days before the war.

John opens his mouth, and closes it. Annie is more alive than she has been in weeks.

JOHN
By the way, Julius, your story doesn't establish what you started out to prove, that a rabbit's foot brings good luck.

JULIUS
Hit's plain 'nuff ter me, suh. I bet young missis dere kin 'splain it herse'f.

ANNIE
(promptly)
I rather suspect that Sis' Becky had no rabbit's foot.

JULIUS
You is hit de bull's-eye de fus' fire, ma'm. Ef Sis' Becky had had a rabbit foot, she nebber would 'a' went th'oo all dis trouble.

John, with a look at the two of them, goes inside for something he has just thought of. He is gone only a moment. When he comes back out onto the piazza, Julius is gone.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - MORNING

SUPER: "SEVERAL WEEKS LATER."

Annie, pink, upright, in a walking-dress, on John's arm. The rockaway waits at the steps. Julius holds the mare's head.

ANNIE
John, I wish you would look in my room, and bring me my handkerchief. You will find it in the pocket of my blue dress.

JOHN
Certainly.

INT. THE VINEYARD - BEDROOM - MORNING

A wardrobe. A blue dress on a hook. John fishes in the pocket and pulls out a handkerchief. Something else comes out with it and falls to the floor. He stoops and picks it up.

It is a rabbit's foot: small, dry, worn, on a loop of string. The Rabbit's Foot.

He looks at it a long time. He looks out the window at the rockaway, where Annie is climbing in, and Julius is handing her up the step, politely, with perfect gravity, looking nowhere.

SFX: a mockingbird, distant, a few notes, not quite a tune

John folds the foot in the handkerchief, goes out, and walks down to the carriage. He hands the handkerchief to his wife. She takes it. Their eyes meet, and something passes between them that neither of them is going to talk about.

He takes his seat. Julius clucks to the mare.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - LANE - DAY

The rockaway rolling down the lane between the elms.

MUSIC: BED IN - bright, plain, major strings

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

Julius, alone on the log, looking at the lens. His left hand is empty. He turns it over and looks at it.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
I mought len' it ter anybody I sot sto' by. I said I would n' sell it. I nebber said I would n' len' it.

He closes the empty hand.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Nex' time we gwine ter dribe by de short road, en I's gwine ter hab ter tell you 'bout a gray mare, en a gray wolf. Dey's two diffe'nt tales, but ef you ax me, dey ain' on'y one.

MUSIC: STING - a single plucked banjo

FADE OUT.

THE END
